Hi there, netlanders,

( sorry, I had to make this a followup as i didn't seem to be able to send
  it the normal way )

I hereby present a method to reduce the diskspace needed for save-files and
bones-files by aproximatelly 50%.

The problem on a micro-computer (in this case Sinclair QL and an Atari ST)
without a hard disk is that given the size of 1 level (about 10-14K), it takes
12*60=720K diskspace to play a decent game of hack. Unfortunately until now I
had to remove all bones-files from our disks and deliberately skip 5 levels
(levels 55-60) to be absolutely sure that our game was not interrupted by
the message:

"Suddenly the dungeon collapses"
"Couldn't write xxxx bytes to file xxxx"

(Yes, we based our QL/ST port on the UN*X version rather than the MesS-DOS one)

A *VERY* frustrating way to end a game, especially when you already have 
the amulet in your hands (This actually happened!).

After a little investigation on how to prevent all this, Felix Croes noted
that the save-, level- and bones-files (to be called save-files from now on)
consisted largely of null bytes. Therefore he introduced a packing scheme that
compressed a multi-byte sequence of zeroes with an abreviation.

A quotation from Felix:

"PACK encodes runs of zeroes of size 2..255 as a two-byte code, 0xFD followed
 by a byte with value 2..255. 0xFD is encoded as 0xFD, 0x00. A single 0xFD at
 the end of the pack buffer (i.e. buf[PBUFSIZE-1] == 0xFD) is also considered
 as an encoding of 0xFD. This implies that the save & load buffers must be of
 the same size.

 PACK could be extended:
   - Don't encode strings of size 2..255, but of size 3..257. Encode runs of
     zeroes as 0xFD followed by a byte with value 1..255 (# zeroes - 2). This
     results in a marginally shorter save file (< 0.1%), I don't think it is
     worth the trouble.
   - Use a code different from 0xFD, which occurs less often in save files.
     However, 0xFD is pretty rare already.
   - Also encode runs of other bytes. The two most obvious candidates are 0x00
     and 0xFF. However, this would complicate the packing algorithm and result
     in save files less than 1% shorter.
   - Use a completely different compression algorithm. The gains in terms of
     disk space could be astonishing, considering the results of using the
     general purpose "compress" program on nethack's save files. However,
     such a compression method would probably take a lot of time, and would
     certainly take a lot of memory space, and nethack is rather bulky as it is.
   - Make save files shorter by saving fewer bytes. This implies switching off
     some #ifdefs, or else compression of the data present in memory.

 Everything considered, I think the present version performs quite reasonable."

This solution costs very little, and on a micro-computer also speeds up the
disk I/O considerably because buffering is used and there is much less to
read or write.

Felix' solution consists of minor changes in the files
	bones.c
	do.c
	lev.c
	save.c
and a couple of extra subroutines which have been placed in 'lev.c'.

The method has been tested on NetHack 2.3e versions on an Atari ST, a 
Sinclair QL and a Gould PN9010 (BSD 4.3).

Below this text are the 4 mentioned files, with all changes clearly marked
with	#ifdef PACKING
	#endif /* PACKING /**/

Absolutely do NOT use the files given here to compile on your system,
they are also modified (mostly unmarked) for other bug fixes and portability
problems, to prevent you from compiling it I deliberately mutilated the
files so that you can't. I suggest that you extract the parts between the
#ifdef PACKING and #endif's and put them in your version of NetHack.
Another reason for giving subroutines instead of patches is that I don't
know the current state of NetHack 2.3; could anyone send me a list of all
bug(fixe)s found so far? Thanks in advance.

The modifications described are only tested on the machines named above,
and only with NetHack 2.3e, although we see no reason for problems if you use
the modifications on another machine or another version of NetHack.

By the way, although config.h is altered you don't need to recompile all of
NetHack again (only bones.c, do.c, lev.c and save.c), so take care when using
"make".

When compiling for MesS-DOS, things will have to be changed (especially the
COUNT stuff), but since we luckily DON'T have such a machine, we were not able
to cater for these scrapheaps also.
"The constant flow of problems seems to suggest that NetHack is completely
 unsuitable for this kind of machine." (F. Croes)


